1
00:00:00,065 --> 00:00:02,370
December 31, 1999.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:04,472
Kandahar, Afghanistan.

3
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:06,880
An Indian minister steps onto a runway

4
00:00:06,891 --> 00:00:08,217
controlled by the Taliban.

5
00:00:08,554 --> 00:00:10,022
With him are three prisoners.

6
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:13,362
A few hours later, the hostages on a

7
00:00:13,373 --> 00:00:15,297
hijacked Indian airliner walk free.

8
00:00:15,615 --> 00:00:16,669
So do the three men.

9
00:00:17,657 --> 00:00:19,472
Within weeks, one of them founds a new

10
00:00:19,483 --> 00:00:20,287
militant group.

11
00:00:20,635 --> 00:00:22,494
It will be blamed for an attack on India's

12
00:00:22,505 --> 00:00:24,559
Parliament, and it will claim the bombing

13
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:25,222
at Pulwama.

14
00:00:25,570 --> 00:00:27,581
The second will be convicted in Pakistan

15
00:00:27,635 --> 00:00:29,124
over the kidnapping of an American

16
00:00:29,135 --> 00:00:29,700
journalist.

17
00:00:30,048 --> 00:00:31,777
The third goes back to recruiting for the

18
00:00:31,787 --> 00:00:32,711
war in Kashmir.

19
00:00:33,695 --> 00:00:35,597
The deal brought every remaining hostage

20
00:00:35,608 --> 00:00:36,326
home alive.

21
00:00:37,371 --> 00:00:39,447
So how does a decision that saved so many

22
00:00:39,458 --> 00:00:40,958
end up costing so many more?

23
00:00:41,940 --> 00:00:43,397
Everything here comes from the public

24
00:00:43,407 --> 00:00:44,897
record — the official account of the

25
00:00:44,907 --> 00:00:47,190
hijacking, Pakistani court rulings, and

26
00:00:47,201 --> 00:00:48,918
India's own terrorism designations.

27
00:00:49,976 --> 00:00:51,846
It begins, like most storms, with

28
00:00:51,856 --> 00:00:52,639
something small.

29
00:00:59,798 --> 00:01:01,635
Christmas Eve, 1999.

30
00:01:01,955 --> 00:01:05,184
Indian Airlines Flight 814, an Airbus

31
00:01:05,194 --> 00:01:07,781
A300, leaves Kathmandu for Delhi.

32
00:01:08,103 --> 00:01:10,103
There are 190 people on board.

33
00:01:11,183 --> 00:01:13,563
Shortly before five in the evening, inside

34
00:01:13,574 --> 00:01:15,987
Indian airspace, five armed men take the

35
00:01:15,998 --> 00:01:16,379
plane.

36
00:01:17,329 --> 00:01:19,840
They belong to Harkat-ul-Mujahideen, a

37
00:01:19,851 --> 00:01:21,622
Pakistan-based militant group.

38
00:01:21,942 --> 00:01:23,703
They order the captain to turn west.

39
00:01:24,646 --> 00:01:26,212
According to the official account, the

40
00:01:26,223 --> 00:01:28,733
plane lands at Amritsar, still in India,

41
00:01:28,886 --> 00:01:29,646
low on fuel.

42
00:01:29,994 --> 00:01:32,223
For about an hour it sits on the tarmac.

43
00:01:32,570 --> 00:01:34,364
It is the one moment the crisis could have

44
00:01:34,375 --> 00:01:35,842
been ended on Indian soil.

45
00:01:36,889 --> 00:01:37,433
It isn't.

46
00:01:37,776 --> 00:01:39,265
Angry at the delay in refuelling,

47
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:41,624
the hijackers stab two passengers.

48
00:01:41,971 --> 00:01:43,765
Then they force the captain to take off,

49
00:01:44,048 --> 00:01:45,797
narrowly missing a fuel tanker on the

50
00:01:45,808 --> 00:01:46,287
runway.

51
00:01:47,239 --> 00:01:47,924
Lahore.

52
00:01:48,265 --> 00:01:50,678
Then Dubai, where 27 passengers are let

53
00:01:50,689 --> 00:01:51,004
go.

54
00:01:51,363 --> 00:01:53,689
With them, the hijackers hand over a body.

55
00:01:54,011 --> 00:01:55,837
Rupin Katyal was 25.

56
00:01:56,146 --> 00:01:57,656
He had been stabbed at Amritsar.

57
00:01:58,735 --> 00:02:00,637
He is the only hostage who will not come

58
00:02:00,648 --> 00:02:00,996
home.

59
00:02:01,979 --> 00:02:03,783
On Christmas morning, the plane lands in

60
00:02:03,783 --> 00:02:05,544
Kandahar — in a country run by the

61
00:02:05,555 --> 00:02:07,631
Taliban, which India does not recognise.

62
00:02:08,697 --> 00:02:10,686
India has no embassy there, and no

63
00:02:10,697 --> 00:02:12,295
relations with the men who run it.

64
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:15,125
Now the only question is the price.

65
00:02:16,298 --> 00:02:18,081
The first demands were enormous.

66
00:02:18,418 --> 00:02:20,505
Thirty-six prisoners from Indian jails.

67
00:02:20,831 --> 00:02:22,603
Two hundred million dollars in cash.

68
00:02:22,950 --> 00:02:24,483
And the body of Sajjad Afghani,

69
00:02:24,983 --> 00:02:27,255
a founder of their group, killed in India.

70
00:02:28,331 --> 00:02:30,005
Over six days on the tarmac, with the

71
00:02:30,016 --> 00:02:32,179
Taliban as go-between, the list shrank.

72
00:02:33,148 --> 00:02:35,050
In the end it came down to three names.

73
00:02:35,372 --> 00:02:37,003
Maulana Masood Azhar.

74
00:02:37,318 --> 00:02:38,916
Ahmed Omar Saeed Sheikh.

75
00:02:39,231 --> 00:02:40,861
Mushtaq Ahmed Zargar.

76
00:02:41,823 --> 00:02:44,595
On December 31, External Affairs Minister

77
00:02:44,606 --> 00:02:46,530
Jaswant Singh flew to Kandahar with all

78
00:02:46,541 --> 00:02:46,932
three.

79
00:02:47,253 --> 00:02:49,329
The exchange was made, and the hostages

80
00:02:49,340 --> 00:02:50,145
were flown home.

81
00:02:50,492 --> 00:02:52,036
According to the official account, the

82
00:02:52,047 --> 00:02:54,036
Taliban then gave the hijackers ten hours

83
00:02:54,047 --> 00:02:56,166
to leave Afghanistan, and drove them, with

84
00:02:56,177 --> 00:02:57,971
the three freed men, towards the border

85
00:02:57,982 --> 00:02:58,677
with Pakistan.

86
00:02:59,742 --> 00:03:01,068
None of them was arrested.

87
00:03:02,421 --> 00:03:04,149
India had bought back its citizens.

88
00:03:05,216 --> 00:03:07,097
The bill would arrive in instalments.

89
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:10,728
Masood Azhar was a Pakistani cleric and a

90
00:03:10,739 --> 00:03:13,043
senior figure in Harkat-ul-Mujahideen.

91
00:03:13,385 --> 00:03:16,211
In February 1994 he travelled to Kashmir

92
00:03:16,211 --> 00:03:18,624
under a false identity, and was arrested

93
00:03:18,635 --> 00:03:19,363
near Anantnag.

94
00:03:20,464 --> 00:03:22,529
His name was at the top of the hijackers'

95
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:23,486
list for a reason.

96
00:03:24,449 --> 00:03:26,362
It was not the first attempt to free him.

97
00:03:26,695 --> 00:03:29,695
In 1995, militants in Kashmir kidnapped

98
00:03:29,706 --> 00:03:30,848
six Western tourists.

99
00:03:31,185 --> 00:03:33,369
Their demands included Azhar's release.

100
00:03:34,425 --> 00:03:35,947
That time, India refused.

101
00:03:37,078 --> 00:03:38,915
In 1999, it did not.

102
00:03:39,229 --> 00:03:41,598
Within weeks of Kandahar, Azhar founded a

103
00:03:41,609 --> 00:03:43,435
new organisation in Pakistan:

104
00:03:43,707 --> 00:03:46,294
Jaish-e-Mohammed, the Army of Mohammed.

105
00:03:46,713 --> 00:03:49,104
He reportedly had help from Pakistan's

106
00:03:49,115 --> 00:03:51,039
intelligence service and from the Taliban.

107
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:53,926
Its name would soon be attached to some of

108
00:03:53,937 --> 00:03:56,067
the worst attacks on India in two decades.

109
00:03:57,231 --> 00:03:59,090
December 13, 2001.

110
00:03:59,438 --> 00:04:01,427
Gunmen storm India's Parliament in New

111
00:04:01,427 --> 00:04:01,786
Delhi.

112
00:04:02,133 --> 00:04:04,068
More than a dozen people die, including

113
00:04:04,079 --> 00:04:04,764
the attackers.

114
00:04:05,086 --> 00:04:07,314
India blamed two Pakistan-based groups:

115
00:04:07,596 --> 00:04:10,086
Lashkar-e-Taiba, and Jaish-e-Mohammed.

116
00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:12,918
What followed was the largest military

117
00:04:12,929 --> 00:04:14,929
build-up on the subcontinent in decades.

118
00:04:15,251 --> 00:04:17,370
India called it Operation Parakram.

119
00:04:17,701 --> 00:04:20,429
By January 2002, around half a million

120
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:22,233
Indian troops were massed on the border.

121
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:24,222
Pakistan answered with hundreds of

122
00:04:24,233 --> 00:04:25,266
thousands of its own.

123
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:28,256
Two nuclear-armed neighbours stood face to

124
00:04:28,267 --> 00:04:29,376
face for ten months.

125
00:04:30,368 --> 00:04:32,835
The standoff eased only after Pakistan's

126
00:04:32,846 --> 00:04:34,520
president, Pervez Musharraf,

127
00:04:35,009 --> 00:04:36,694
pledged under American pressure to end

128
00:04:36,705 --> 00:04:38,009
cross-border infiltration.

129
00:04:39,078 --> 00:04:41,024
Two years after the runway, the group

130
00:04:41,035 --> 00:04:42,948
founded by one of the three freed men had

131
00:04:42,959 --> 00:04:44,687
been blamed for bringing two armies to the

132
00:04:44,698 --> 00:04:45,100
edge.

133
00:04:46,254 --> 00:04:47,863
Omar Sheikh was born in Britain.

134
00:04:48,178 --> 00:04:49,874
He studied at the London School of

135
00:04:49,885 --> 00:04:50,580
Economics.

136
00:04:51,639 --> 00:04:54,487
Then, in 1994, he was arrested in India

137
00:04:54,498 --> 00:04:56,248
over the kidnapping of Western tourists.

138
00:04:56,574 --> 00:04:58,465
The aim of that kidnapping was to force

139
00:04:58,476 --> 00:04:59,976
the release of jailed militants.

140
00:05:00,324 --> 00:05:02,530
Five years later, other men hijacked a

141
00:05:02,541 --> 00:05:04,074
plane to force the release of him.

142
00:05:05,044 --> 00:05:07,761
After Kandahar, he surfaced in Pakistan.

143
00:05:08,103 --> 00:05:09,440
In January 2002,

144
00:05:09,951 --> 00:05:10,624
Daniel Pearl,

145
00:05:11,092 --> 00:05:12,559
a reporter for The Wall Street Journal,

146
00:05:13,037 --> 00:05:14,407
was kidnapped in Karachi while

147
00:05:14,418 --> 00:05:15,983
investigating militant networks.

148
00:05:16,309 --> 00:05:17,157
Pearl was killed.

149
00:05:18,128 --> 00:05:20,258
A Pakistani anti-terrorism court convicted

150
00:05:20,269 --> 00:05:21,921
Sheikh that year and sentenced him to

151
00:05:21,932 --> 00:05:22,345
death.

152
00:05:22,676 --> 00:05:24,371
Then the case began to unravel.

153
00:05:24,693 --> 00:05:27,334
In 2020, a high court overturned his

154
00:05:27,345 --> 00:05:28,204
murder conviction.

155
00:05:28,519 --> 00:05:31,987
In January 2021, Pakistan's Supreme Court

156
00:05:31,998 --> 00:05:32,758
acquitted him.

157
00:05:33,824 --> 00:05:35,139
According to the Committee to Protect

158
00:05:35,150 --> 00:05:36,954
Journalists, he was moved to a government

159
00:05:36,976 --> 00:05:39,020
"safe house" — under guard, but off death

160
00:05:39,031 --> 00:05:39,292
row.

161
00:05:40,465 --> 00:05:42,085
The third man is the least known.

162
00:05:42,407 --> 00:05:44,928
Mushtaq Ahmed Zargar is from Srinagar.

163
00:05:45,367 --> 00:05:46,824
He founded a militant group called

164
00:05:46,824 --> 00:05:48,802
Al-Umar Mujahideen, and he had been in

165
00:05:48,813 --> 00:05:50,693
Indian jails since 1992.

166
00:05:51,688 --> 00:05:53,579
After Kandahar, he did not reappear in the

167
00:05:53,590 --> 00:05:54,177
headlines.

168
00:05:54,497 --> 00:05:56,997
He went to work quietly — from Pakistan,

169
00:05:57,269 --> 00:05:59,236
and from Illegally Occupied Pakistani

170
00:05:59,247 --> 00:05:59,823
Kashmir.

171
00:06:00,166 --> 00:06:03,058
In April 2022, India's home ministry

172
00:06:03,069 --> 00:06:05,025
formally designated him a terrorist.

173
00:06:05,384 --> 00:06:07,047
It said Zargar had been "running an

174
00:06:07,058 --> 00:06:09,112
incessant campaign from Pakistan to fuel

175
00:06:09,123 --> 00:06:10,873
militancy in Jammu and Kashmir."

176
00:06:11,955 --> 00:06:13,847
Twenty-two years after the runway, he was

177
00:06:13,857 --> 00:06:14,575
still at it.

178
00:06:15,716 --> 00:06:17,683
Jaish-e-Mohammed never went away.

179
00:06:18,025 --> 00:06:19,633
In January 2016,

180
00:06:20,090 --> 00:06:21,894
attackers hit the Pathankot air base —

181
00:06:22,416 --> 00:06:24,307
an attack India laid at Azhar's door.

182
00:06:24,655 --> 00:06:27,177
Then came February 14, 2019.

183
00:06:27,525 --> 00:06:28,818
On the highway at Pulwama,

184
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:31,492
a suicide bomber drove into a convoy of

185
00:06:31,503 --> 00:06:33,133
the Central Reserve Police Force.

186
00:06:33,481 --> 00:06:34,905
Forty personnel were killed.

187
00:06:35,946 --> 00:06:37,685
Jaish-e-Mohammed claimed it.

188
00:06:38,636 --> 00:06:41,104
Twelve days later, Indian jets struck what

189
00:06:41,115 --> 00:06:43,169
India said was a Jaish-e-Mohammed training

190
00:06:43,180 --> 00:06:45,571
